<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-fem-mc-det" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Deterministic Analysis</title>
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <p>
        In this section, we present a preliminary error analysis under the simplifying assumption that <m>\mathcal{S}</m> is computed exactly. We define the norms used throughout, before establishing well-posedness through verifying the conditions for the Lax-Milgram theorem. We then derive separate bounds for the discretisation error <m>\enorm{u-u_h}</m> and the iteration error <m>\enorm{u_h-u_h^{(n)}}</m>, which combine via the triangle inequality to give the main deterministic estimate. For simplicity, we take <m>g=0</m> (meaning <m>u\in H_0^1</m>) from this section onwards - an extension to nonhomogenous inflow data is left to future work.
    </p>  
</subsection>

<subsection>
    <title>Norms</title>
    <p>
        We equip <m>V_h</m> with the SUPG norm, defined as
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-supg-norm">
            \label{eqn:supg-norm}
            \enorm{v}^2 := \mu\ltwo{v}^2 
            + \sum_{K\in\mathscr{T}_h} \delta_K \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v}^2
            + \int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}| v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S,
        </md>
        where <m>\mu</m> is the positivity constant defined in<xref ref="eqn-positivity"/> and <m>\delta_K</m> is the element-wise stabilisation parameter.
        This immediately results in the following relation of norms, used frequently in later analysis
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-rel-norms">
            \label{eqn:rel-norms}
            \enorm{v}^2 \geq \mu\ltwo{v}^2 \qquad \forall v\in V_h.
        </md>
        For continuity estimates, we also require the stronger star-SUPG norm, given by
        <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-star-supg-norm">
            \label{eqn:star-supg-norm}
            \snorm{v}^2 = \enorm{v}^2 + \sum_{K\in\mathscr{T}} \delta_K^{-1} \ltwok{v}^2.
        </md>
    </p>
</subsection>


<subsection>
    <title>Well-Posedness and Key Properties</title>
    <p>
        We now present key properties of the bilinear and linear forms defined in<xref ref="eqn-a"/>,<xref ref="eqn-s"/> and<xref ref="eqn-l"/>. This will show that the variational problem<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/> is well-posed, and provide the necessary results for error analysis.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-well-posed">
        <title>Consistency of the SUPG Method</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>u</m> be the true solution of<xref ref="eqn-strong-simple"/>. Then for all <m>v\in V_h</m> we have 
                <md number="yes">
                    a(u,v) = s(u,v) + l(v).
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            We have 
            <md number="yes">
                <mrow>
                    a(u,v) - s(u,v) - l(v) &amp;
                    \int_{\Omega} (\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla u + \sigma u - \mathcal{S}u - q) v \, \mathrm{d}x
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                    &amp;+ \sum_{K\in\mathscr{T}_h} \int_K \delta_K (\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla u + \sigma u - \mathcal{S}u - q)(\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v) \, \mathrm{d}x
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                    &amp;- \int_{\Gamma_-} (\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n})(u-g)v\, ds
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                    =&amp; 0.
                </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </proof>
    



<lemma xml:id="lem-a-coer">
    <title>Coercivity of <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    For all <m>v\in V_h</m> we have 
    <md number="yes">
        a(v,v) \geq \frac{1}{2}\enorm{v}^2.
    </md>    
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<proof><p>
    We treat each term in<xref ref="eqn-a"/> separately.
    For the convection term, by Green's formula<xref ref="eqn-greens"/> we have
    <md number="yes">
            <mrow>\int_{\Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, v \, \mathrm{d}x 
            &amp;= \frac{1}{2} \int_{\Omega} \mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla (v^2)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \frac{1}{2} \int_{\partial \Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S .</mrow> 
    </md>
    We combine this with the boundary inflow term. Let <m>\Gamma_+ = \partial\Omega\setminus\Gamma_-</m>. We have
    <md number="yes">
            <mrow>\frac{1}{2} \int_{\partial \Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S 
            - \int_{\Gamma_-} (\mathbf{b} &amp;\cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S </mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \frac{1}{2}\int_{\Gamma_+} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S 
            - \frac{1}{2}\int_{\Gamma_-} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S </mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \frac{1}{2}\int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}|\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S,</mrow>
    </md>
    where the last equality follows from definition of <m>\Gamma_-</m> in<xref ref="eqn-strong-bcs"/>.
    Combined with the reaction term, we have
    <md number="yes">
            <mrow>\int_{\Omega} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, v + \sigma v^2\, \mathrm{d}x 
            - \int_{\Gamma_-} (\mathbf{b} &amp;\cdot \mathbf{n})\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \int_{\Omega} \sigma v^2\, \mathrm{d}x 
            + \frac{1}{2}\int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}|\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S</mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-coer-a-1">&amp;\geq \mu\ltwo{v}^2 + \frac{1}{2}\int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}|\, v^2 \, \mathrm{d}S.</mrow>
        </md>
    Now, we turn to the SUPG term. We have
    <md number="yes">
            <mrow>\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v &amp;+ \sigma v)\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;=
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h} 
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, |\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v |^2\, \mathrm{d}x
            + \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h} \int_K \delta_K \sigma v\, (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, dx</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;=
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h} \delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2
            + \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h} \int_K \delta_K \sigma v\, (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, dx</mrow>
    </md>
    The second term is indefinite, and we bound its contribution with Young's inequality<xref ref="eqn-youngs"/>
    <md number="yes" >
        \left| \int_K \delta_K \sigma v\, (\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v)\, dx\right|
        \leq
        \frac{1}{2}\,\delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2
+ \frac{1}{2}\,\delta_K \linf{\sigma}^2 \|v\|_{L^2(K)}^2.
    </md>
    Thus, the SUPG Term is bounded below by
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v &amp;+ \sigma v)\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\geq 
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h} \delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2
            -
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \frac{1}{2}\,\delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\qquad \qquad - 
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \frac{1}{2}\,\delta_K \linf{\sigma}^2 \|v\|_{L^2(K)}^2</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-coer-a-2">
            &amp;=
            \frac{1}{2}
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2 
            - 
            \frac{1}{2}
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \linf{\sigma}^2 \|v\|_{L^2(K)}^2.</mrow>
    </md>
    By<xref ref="eqn-delta-bound"/>, we have <m>\delta_K \leq \mu/\linf{\sigma}^2</m>. Thus,
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v &amp;+ \sigma v)\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-coer-a-3">
            &amp;\geq 
            \frac{1}{2}
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2 
            - 
            \frac{\mu}{2}\ltwo{v}^2</mrow>
    </md>
    Adding<xref ref="eqn-coer-a-1"/> and<xref ref="eqn-coer-a-3"/>, we have
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            a(v, v) 
            &amp;\geq \frac{\mu}{2} \|v\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2
            + \frac{1}{2} \sum_K \delta_K \|\mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla v\|_{L^2(K)}^2
            + \frac{1}{2} \| |\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}|^{1/2} v\|_{L^2(\partial\Omega)}^2</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\geq \frac{1}{2} \enorm{v}^2.</mrow>
    </md>
</p></proof>
     


<lemma xml:id="lem-a-cont">
    <title>Continuity of <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    For all <m>w,v\in V_h</m> we have that
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-a-cont">
        a(w,v) \leq C_a \snorm{w} \enorm{v},
    </md>
    where <m>C_a = 5 + \linf{\sigma}/\mu</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<proof><p>
    We bound every term of<xref ref="eqn-a"/> in the form of<xref ref="eqn-a-cont"/>.
    Beginning with the convection term, we apply Green's formula
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-a-cont-01">
            \int_\Omega (\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla w)\,v\,\mathrm{d}x
            =
            \int_{\partial\Omega} (\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n})\,wv\,\mathrm{d}S
            -
            \int_\Omega (\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v)\,w\,\mathrm{d}x.
    </md>
    We bound the first term in<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-01"/> as
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \left|
                \int_{\partial\Omega} (\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n})\,wv\,\mathrm{d}S
            \right|
            &amp;\leq
            \int_{\partial\Omega} 
            |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|\,|w||v|\,
            \mathrm{d}S\</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \left|\int_{\partial\Omega} 
            |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|
            \,w^2\,\mathrm{d}S\right|^{1/2}
            \left|\int_{\partial\Omega} 
            |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|
            \,v^2\,\mathrm{d}S\right|^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \enorm{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \snorm{w}
            \enorm{v}.</mrow>
    </md>
    The second term in<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-01"/> is bounded as
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \left|
                \int_\Omega (\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v)\,w\,\mathrm{d}x
            \right|
            &amp;\leq
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \left|\int_K (\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v)\,w\,\mathrm{d}x\right|</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v} \ltwok{w}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq            
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K^{1/2}\ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v} 
            \delta_K^{-1/2}\ltwok{w}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \left(\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v}^2 \right)^{1/2}
            \left(\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K^{-1} \ltwok{w}^2\right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \enorm{v}\snorm{w}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Thus, the convection term is bounded as
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-a-cont-1">
            \left|\int_\Omega (\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla w)\,v\,\mathrm{d}x\right|
            \leq
            2\snorm{w}\enorm{v}.
    </md>
    The reaction term is bounded by
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \label{eqn:a-cont-2}
            \left| \int_{\Omega} \sigma v w \, dx \right|
            &amp;\leq \linf{\sigma} \|w\|_{L^2} \|v\|_{L^2}</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-a-cont-2">
            &amp;\leq \frac{\linf{\sigma}}{\mu}\snorm{w}\enorm{v}.</mrow>
    </md>
    The inflow boundary term is bounded by
    <md number="yes">
            <mrow>
            \left| \int_{\Gamma_-} (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}) \, w v \, \mathrm{d}S \right|
            &amp;\leq 
            \int_{\Gamma_-} 
            |\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{n}|
            \, |w| |v| \, \mathrm{d}S</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \left(
                \int_{\Gamma_-} |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|\,w^2\,\mathrm{d}S
            \right)^{1/2}
            \left(
                \int_{\Gamma_-} |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|\,v^2\,\mathrm{d}S
            \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \left(
                \int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|\,w^2\,\mathrm{d}S
            \right)^{1/2}
            \left(
                \int_{\partial\Omega} |\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|\,v^2\,\mathrm{d}S
            \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \enorm{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-a-cont-3">
            &amp;\leq
            \snorm{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow>
    </md>
    For the SUPG term, we have
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla w &amp;+ \sigma w)\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla w)\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-supg-continuity">
            &amp; \qquad\qquad+
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, \sigma w\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x.</mrow>
    </md>
    We bound the first RHS term of<xref ref="eqn-supg-continuity"/> as
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            &amp;\left|\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}            
            \int_K\delta_K  
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla w)\,(\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x
            \right|</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\qquad\qquad\leq
            \left(\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla w}^2 \right)^{1/2}
            \left(\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot \nabla v}^2 \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-a-cont-4">
            &amp;\qquad\qquad\leq
            \snorm{w}\enorm{v}</mrow>
    </md>
    we bound the second term in<xref ref="eqn-supg-continuity"/> as
    <md number="yes" ><mrow>
            \left|\sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K
            \delta_K \, \sigma w\,
            (\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x\right|
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma}
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K
            \ltwok{w}\ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma}
            \left(
                \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
                \delta_K
                \ltwok{w}^2
            \right)^{1/2}
            \left(
                \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
                \delta_K
                \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v}^2
            \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma}
            \delta_\text{max}^{1/2} 
            \ltwo{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma}
            \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}}
            \snorm{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-a-cont-5">
            &amp;\leq
            \snorm{w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow>
    </md>
    Adding together<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-1"/>,<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-2"/>,<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-3"/>,<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-4"/> and<xref ref="eqn-a-cont-5"/>, we have
    <md number="yes" >
        a(w,v) 
        \leq
        \left(5 + \frac{\linf{\sigma}}{\mu}\right)
        \snorm{w}
        \enorm{v}
    </md>
</p></proof>

<lemma xml:id="lem-bound-big-s">
    <title>Boundedness of <m>\mathcal{S}</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    For all <m>v\in H^1(\Omega)</m> we have that
    <md number="yes">
        \ltwo{\mathcal{S}v} \leq \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)} \ltwo{v},
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<proof><p>
    By Cauchy-Schwarz, we have
    <md number="yes" >
        \left| \int_{\Omega} \mathcal{K}(x,y) v(y)\, \mathrm{d}y \right|^2
        \leq
        \left( \int_{\Omega} |\mathcal{K}(x,y)|^2 \, \mathrm{d}y \right) \|v\|_{L^2}^2.
    </md>
    Thus, by definition of <m>\mathcal{S}</m><xref ref="eqn-def-big-s"/>,
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}v} 
            &amp;= \ltwo{\sigma_T(x)\int_\Omega \mathcal{K}(x,y) v(y)\mathrm{d}y}\\
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma_T} 
            \left(
            \int_\Omega 
            \left| \int_{\Omega} \mathcal{K}(x,y) v(y)\, \mathrm{d}y \right|^2
            \mathrm{d}x
            \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            \linf{\sigma_T} 
            \left(
                \int_\Omega 
                \int_\Omega 
                \left| \mathcal{K}(x,y) \right|^2
                \mathrm{d}x
                \mathrm{d}y
            \right)^{1/2}
            \,\ltwo{v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;= 
            \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)}
            \ltwo{v}.</mrow>
    </md>
</p></proof>

<lemma xml:id="lem-s-cont">
    <title>Continuity of <m>s(\cdot,\cdot)</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    For all <m>w,v\in V_h</m> we have that
    <md number="yes" >
        s(w,v) \leq \tilde{C}_s \ltwo{w} \enorm{v},
    </md>
    and thus (by relation of norms),
    <md number="yes" >
        s(w,v) \leq C_s \enorm{w} \enorm{v}.
    </md>
    where 
    <md number="yes" >
        C_s = \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}}\tilde{C}_s = \linf{\sigma_T}\ldouble{\mathcal{K}}\frac{1}{\mu}\left(1 + \mu^{1/2}\delta_\text{max}^{1/2}\right)
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<proof><p>
    We have 
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            s(w,v) 
            &amp;=
            \int_{\Omega} (\mathcal{S}w)\, v \, \mathrm{d}x
            + \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \int_K \delta_K (\mathcal{S}w)\,(\boldsymbol{b}\cdot\nabla v)\, \mathrm{d}x </mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}\ltwo{v}
            +
            \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
            \delta_K
            \ltwok{\mathcal{S}w}\ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}
            \ltwo{v} 
            + 
            \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}
            \left(
                \sum_{K \in \mathscr{T}_h}
                \delta_K
                \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v}^2
            \right)^{1/2}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}
            \ltwo{v} 
            +
            \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}
            \enorm{v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \ltwo{\mathcal{S}w}
            \left(
                \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}} + \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \right)\enorm{v}\\
            \text{by Lemma <xref ref="lem-bound-big-s"/>, }</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)}
            \left(
                \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}} + \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \right)\ltwo{w}\enorm{v}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)}
            \left(
                \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}} + \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \right)
            \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}}
            \enorm{w}\enorm{v}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Using<xref ref="eqn-delta-bound"/>, we take
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            \tilde{C}_s &amp;= \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)}
            \left(
                \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}} + \delta_\text{max}^{1/2}
            \right)</mrow><mrow>
            C_s &amp;= \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}}\tilde{C}_s</mrow>
    </md>
</p></proof>



<corollary xml:id="cor-a-s-coer">
    <title>Coercivity of <m>a-s</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Suppose
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-a-s-coer-1">
        \label{eqn:a-s-coer-1}<!--
        % \linf{\sigma_T}\|\mathcal{K}\|_{L^2(\Omega\times\Omega)}
        % \left(
        %     \frac{1}{\mu} +\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}\linf{\sigma}}
        % \right)-->
        C_s
        \leq
        \frac{1}{2}.
    </md>
    Then the bilinear form <m>a-s</m> is coercive. That is, there exists an <m>\alpha>0</m> such that for all <m>v\in V_h</m>
    <md number="yes" >
        a(v,v) - s(v,v) \geq \alpha \enorm{v}^2.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<proof><p>
    By <xref ref="lem-a-coer"/> and <xref ref="lem-s-cont"/>, we have
    <md number="yes" >
            a(v,v)-s(v,v) 
            \geq
            \frac{1}{2}\enorm{v}^2 - C_s\enorm{v}\enorm{v}.
    </md>
    By the assumption<xref ref="eqn-a-s-coer-1"/>, we have
    <md number="yes" >
        C_s \leq \frac{1}{2}.
    </md>
    Therefore, taking <m>\alpha = 1/2 - C_s</m>,
    <md number="yes" >
        a(v,v) - s(v,v) \geq \alpha \enorm{v}^2.
    </md>
</p></proof>

<corollary xml:id="cor-a-s-cont">
    <title>Continuity of <m>a-s</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    For all <m>w,v\in V_h</m>, we have that
    <md number="yes" >
        a(w,v) - s(w,v) \leq C_A \snorm{w}\enorm{v},
    </md>
    where <m>C_A = C_a + C_s</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<proof><p>
    By <xref ref="lem-s-cont"/> and <xref ref="lem-a-cont"/>, we have
    <md number="yes" >
        a(w,v) - s(w,v) \leq C_a \snorm{w}\enorm{v} + C_s\enorm{w}\enorm{v} \leq (C_a + C_s)\snorm{w}\enorm{v}.
    </md>
</p></proof>

<theorem xml:id="thm-well-posed">
    <title>Well-posedness</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>g\in H^{1/2}(\partial\Omega)</m> with an extension <m>\hat{g}\in H^1(\Omega)</m> such that <m>\hat{g}|_{\partial\Omega} = g</m>. Then the variational problem of finding <m>u\in H^1(\Omega)</m> such that <m>u = g</m> on <m>\Gamma_-</m> and
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-lax-2">
        a(u,v) - s(u,v) = l(v) \qquad \forall v\in H_0^1(\Omega)
    </md>
    has a unique solution.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<proof><p>
    Define <m>\tilde{u} = u - \hat{g}\in H_0^1(\Omega)</m>. We seek 
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-lax-1">
        \label{eqn:lax-1}
        a(\tilde{u},v) - s(\tilde{u},v) = \tilde{l}(v) \qquad \forall v\in H_0^1(\Omega)
    </md>
    where
    <md number="yes" >
        \tilde{l}(v) = l(v) - a(\hat{g},v) + s(\hat{g}, v).
    </md>
    By <xref ref="cor-a-s-coer"/> and <xref ref="cor-a-s-cont"/>, we have that <m>a-s</m> is coercive and bounded. Moreover, <m>\tilde{l}</m> is controlled by the SUPG-norm, with each term bounded since <m>q\in L^2(\Omega)</m> and <m>\hat{g}\in H^1(\Omega)</m>.

    Therefore, the Lax-Milgram theorem applies to give a unique <m>\tilde{u}</m> solving<xref ref="eqn-lax-1"/>, hence a unique <m>u = \tilde{u}+\hat{g}</m> solving<xref ref="eqn-lax-2"/>.
</p></proof>


</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-fem-mc-fe-det">
    <title>Finite Element Error</title>
    <p>
        We consider the first term in the triangle inequality in<xref ref="eqn-triangle-ineq"/>. That is, we find an error estimate and convergence rate for the theoretical finite element solution <m>u_h\in V_h</m> of<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. 
    </p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-best-approx">
    <title>C\'{e}a-type best-approximation estimate</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>u\in H^1_0</m> be a solution of<xref ref="eqn-strong"/> with <m>g=0</m> and let <m>u_h\in V_h</m> be a solution of<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. Then we have the following best approximation result:
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u - u_h}
        \leq 
        \left(1 + \frac{C_A}{\alpha}\right)
        \inf_{w_h \in V_h} 
        \snorm{u - w_h}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<proof><p>
    Let <m>w_h\in V_h</m> be arbitrary, we split the error as
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-best-appro-1">
        \label{eqn:best-appro-1}
        u - u_h
        = (u - w_h)
        + \underbrace{(w_h - u_h)}_{\phi_h \in V_h}.
    </md>
    Applying coercivity to <m>A = a-s</m> (<xref ref="cor-a-s-coer"/>), we have
    <md number="yes" >
        \alpha \enorm{\phi_h}^2
        \leq
        A(\phi_h, \phi_h) = A(w_h-u_h, \phi_h).
    </md>
    We insert the exact solution <m>u</m> into the first slot,
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            A(w_h-u_h, \phi_h)
            &amp;=
            A(w_h-u + u-u_h, \phi_h)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            A(w_h-u, \phi_h) + A(u-u_h, \phi_h)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            A(w_h-u, \phi_h),</mrow>
    </md>
    where the last equality is due to Galerkin orthogonality.
    By continuity (<xref ref="cor-a-s-cont"/>), we have
    <md number="yes">
            \alpha \enorm{\phi_h}^2
            \leq
            A(w_h-u, \phi_h)
            \leq
            C_A\snorm{w_h - u} \enorm{\phi_h}.
    </md>
    Thus,
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{\phi_h} \leq \frac{C_A}{\alpha}\snorm{w_h - u}.
    </md>
    Applying the triangle inequality to<xref ref="eqn-best-appro-1"/>, 
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            \enorm{u-u_h}
            &amp;\leq
            \enorm{u - w_h}
            +
            \enorm{\phi_h}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \snorm{u - w_h}
            +
            \frac{C_A}{\alpha}\snorm{w_h - u}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            \left(
                1 + \frac{C_A}{\alpha}
            \right)\snorm{w_h - u}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Taking the infimum over <m>w_h\in V_h</m>, we have the result
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u - u_h}
        \leq 
        \left(1 + \frac{C_A}{\alpha}\right)
        \inf_{w_h \in V_h} 
        \snorm{u - w_h}.
    </md>
</p></proof>

<!--% <proof><p>
%     Let <m>e = u-u_h</m>, take <m>v_h\in V_h</m> arbitrarily. By <xref ref="lem-a-s-coer"/>,
%     <md number="yes"><mrow>
%             \alpha \enorm{e}^2
%             &amp;\leq
%             a(e,e) - s(e,e)</mrow>
%             &amp;= 
%             a(u-v_h+v_h-u_h,e) - s(u-v_h+v_h-u_h,e)\\
%             &amp;=
%             a(u-v_h,e) + a(v_h-u_h,e)\\
%             &amp; \qquad \qquad - s(u-v_h,e) - s(v_h-u_h,e)\\
%             \text{By Galerkin orthogonality,}\\
%             &amp;=
%             a(u-v_h,e) - s(u-v_h,e)\\
%             &amp;\leq
%             C\snorm{u-v_h}\enorm{e}.
%         \end{aligned}
%     </md>
%     Thus, for all <m>v_h\in V_h</m> we have
%     <md number="yes" >
%         \enorm{e} \leq \frac{2C}{\alpha} \snorm{u - v_h}.
%     </md>
%     Taking the infimum over <m>v_h\in V_h</m>, we have
%     <md number="yes" >
%         \enorm{e} \leq \frac{2C}{\alpha} \inf_{v_h\in V_h}\snorm{u-v_h}.
%     </md>
% </p></proof>-->

<corollary xml:id="cor-fe-conv-rate">
    <title>Rates of Convergence</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>k\geq 1</m> denote the piecewise polynomial degree of elements of <m>V_h</m>.
    Suppose the exact solution <m>u\in H_0^r(\Omega)</m> of<xref ref="eqn-strong"/> is such that <m>r</m> is sufficiently large for <m>u</m> to belong to the domain of the Lagrange interpolation operator. Then, there exists a constant <m>C</m> such that
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u-u_h} \leq C h^{\min\{k+1,r\} - 1/2} |u|_{H^r(\Omega)}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<proof><p>
    The proof follows that of~\cite{ashby2025nodally}, Corollary 3.5; we provide a brief overview.
    Denote by <m>\mathcal{I}u\in V_h</m> the piecewise polynomial Lagrange interpolant of <m>u</m> onto the finite element space <m>V_h</m>.
    Taking <m>v_h = \mathcal{I}u</m> in <xref ref="thm-best-approx"/>, we have
    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
            \enorm{u-u_h}^2
            &amp;\leq
            \snorm{u-\mathcal{I}u}^2 </mrow><mrow>
            &amp;= \mu \ltwo{u-\mathcal{I}u}^2
            + \sum_{K\in\mathscr{T}} \delta_K \ltwok{\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla (u-\mathcal{I}u)}^2</mrow><mrow xml:id="eqn-fe-conv-1">
            &amp;\qquad \qquad + \||\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|^{1/2}(u-\mathcal{I}u)\|_{L^2(\partial\Omega)}^2
            + \sum_{K\in\mathscr{T}} \delta_K^{-1}\ltwok{u-\mathcal{I}u}^2.</mrow>
    </md>
    Each of these terms are bounded using standard interpolation estimates~\cite{ern2021finite}, which apply to norms over <m>L^2(\Omega)</m> and <m>L^2(K)</m>. The third term is first bounded by a trace estimate (see e.g.~\cite{evans2022partial}, Section 5.5) in terms of these norms. The following estimates combine to give<xref ref="eqn-fe-conv-1"/>:
    <md number="yes" >
        \|u - \mathcal{I}u\|_{L^2(\Omega)} \leq C_I \, h_K^r \, |u|_{H^r(\Omega)},
    </md>

    <md number="yes" >
        \left\| \delta_K^{1/2} \, \mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla (u - \mathcal{I}u) \right\|_{L^2(K)}
        \leq C_I \, \|\mathbf{b}\|_{\infty,K} \, C_\delta^{1/2} \, h_K^{r - \tfrac{1}{2}} \, |u|_{H^r(\Omega)}, 
    </md>

    <md number="yes">
        <mrow>
             \left\||\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{n}|(u-\mathcal{I}u)\right\|_{L^2(\partial\Omega\cap K)}
            &amp;\leq C_{\mathrm{tr}} \left( h_K^{-1/2} \|u - Iu\|_{L^2(K)} + h_K^{1/2} \|\nabla (u - Iu)\|_{L^2(K)} \right)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq 2 C_{\mathrm{tr}} C_I \, h_K^{r - \tfrac{1}{2}} \, |u|_{H^r(\Omega)},</mrow>
    </md>

    <md number="yes" >
        \delta_K^{-1/2} \, \|u - \mathcal{I}u\|_{L^2(K)}
        \leq C_I \, C_\delta^{-1/2} \, h_K^{r - \tfrac{1}{2}} \, |u|_{H^r(\Omega)}.
    </md>
    where <m>C_{\mathrm{tr}}</m> and <m>C_\mathcal{I}</m> are constants for trace and interpolation estimates, respectively.
</p></proof>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-fem-mc-iter-det">
    <title>Fixed Point Error</title>
    <p>
        Now, we turn to the second error term in the triangle inequality<xref ref="eqn-triangle-ineq"/>. That is, we now derive a convergence rate for <m>\enorm{u_h - u_h^{(n)}}</m>. In doing so, we also find a rate in the <m>L^2(\Omega)</m>-norm.
    </p>
    
<lemma xml:id="lem-cont-mix">
    <title>Contraction in mixed norms</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>u_h\in V_h</m> be the solution of the stationary finite element problem<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. Take <m>u_h^{(0)}\in V_h</m> and denote by <m>\{u_h^{(n)}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0}</m> the sequence of solutions to the iterative problem<xref ref="eqn-iter-problem"/>. We have that, for all <m>n\in \mathbb{N}_0</m>
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u_h-u_h^{(n)}} 
        \leq
        2\tilde{C}_s 
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(n-1)}}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>
<proof><p>
    Let <m>e = u_h - u_h^{(n)}</m>
    By coercivity of <m>a(\cdot,\cdot)</m> (<xref ref="lem-a-coer"/>), 
    <md number="yes"><mrow>
            \frac{1}{2}\enorm{e}^2
            &amp;\leq
            a(e,e)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            a(u_h,e) - a(u_h^{(n)},e)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            s(u_h,e) + l(e) - s(u_h^{(n-1)},e) - l(e)</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;=
            s(u_h-u_h^{(n-1)},e)</mrow><mrow>
            \text{by <xref ref="lem-s-cont"/>,}</mrow><mrow>
            &amp;\leq
            \tilde{C}_s \ltwo{u_h-u_h^{(n-1)}}\enorm{e}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Thus,
    <md number="yes">
        \enorm{e} 
        \leq
        2\tilde{C}_s 
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(n-1)}}.
    </md>
</p></proof>

<theorem xml:id="thm-conv-l2">
    <title>Convergence in <m>L^2(\Omega)</m>-norm</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>u_h\in V_h</m> be the solution of the stationary finite element problem<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. Take <m>u_h^{(0)}\in V_h</m> and denote by <m>\{u_h^{(n)}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0}</m> the sequence of solutions to the iterative problem<xref ref="eqn-iter-problem"/>. We have that, for all <m>n\in \mathbb{N}_0</m>
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-conv-l2">
        \label{eqn:conv-l2}
        \ltwo{u_h-u_h^{(n)}} 
        \leq
        \left(
            \frac{2\tilde{C}_s}{\mu^{1/2}}
        \right)^n
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(0)}}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<proof><p>
    By the relation of norms<xref ref="eqn-rel-norms"/>, we have
    <md number="yes" >
        \ltwo{u_h-u_h^{(n)}} \leq \frac{1}{\mu^{1/2}}\enorm{u_h-u_h^{(n)} }.
    </md>
    Applying <xref ref="lem-cont-mix"/> gives
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-cont-l2">
        \label{eqn:cont-l2}
        \ltwo{u_h-u_h^{(n)}} 
        \leq
        \frac{2\tilde{C}_s}{\mu^{1/2}}
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(n-1)}}.
    </md>
    Iterating<xref ref="eqn-cont-l2"/> yields the desired convergence result.
</p></proof>


<corollary xml:id="cor-conv-supg">
    <title>Convergence in SUPG-norm</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>u_h\in V_h</m> be the solution of the stationary finite element problem<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. Take <m>u_h^{(0)}\in V_h</m> and denote by <m>\{u_h^{(n)}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0}</m> the sequence of solutions to the iterative problem<xref ref="eqn-iter-problem"/>. We have that, for all <m>n\in \mathbb{N}_0</m>
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u_h-u_h^{(n)}} 
        \leq
        2\tilde{C}_s
        \left(
            \frac{2\tilde{C}_s}{\mu^{1/2}}
        \right)^{n-1}
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(0)}}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<proof><p>
    The result follows from combining <xref ref="lem-cont-mix"/> and <xref ref="thm-conv-l2"/>.
</p></proof>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-fem-mc-combined-det">
    <title>Combined Error Estimate</title>
    <p>
We can now populate the triangle inequality for iterative solution error originally proposed in<xref ref="eqn-triangle-ineq"/>.
    </p>



<theorem xml:id="thm-det">
    <title>Error Estimate in the Deterministic Setting</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>u</m> be the solution of<xref ref="eqn-strong"/> and suppose <m>u_h\in V_h</m> is the solution of the stationary finite element problem<xref ref="eqn-fe-problem"/>. Take <m>u_h^{(0)}\in V_h</m> and denote by <m>\{u_h^{(n)}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0}</m> the sequence of solutions to the iterative problem<xref ref="eqn-iter-problem"/>. We have that, for all <m>n\in \mathbb{N}_0</m>
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u-u_h^{(n)}}
        \leq
        C h^{\min\{k+1,r\} - 1/2} |u|_{H^r(\Omega)}
        +
        2\tilde{C}_s
        \left(
            \frac{2\tilde{C}_s}{\mu^{1/2}}
        \right)^{n-1}
        \ltwo{u_h - u_h^{(0)}}.
    </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<proof><p>
    By the triangle inequality, 
    <md number="yes" >
        \enorm{u-u_h^{(n)}}
        \leq
        \enorm{u-u_h}
        +
        \enorm{u_h-u_h^{(n)}},
    </md>
    and the result follows from applying <xref ref="cor-conv-supg"/> and <xref ref="cor-fe-conv-rate"/>.
</p></proof>

</subsection>

<subsection>
    <title>Discussion</title>
    <p>
The analysis above identifies two key constraints on 
the parameters of the problem.        
    </p>

    <p>
First, the combined coercivity result for <m>a - s</m> 
(<xref ref="cor-a-s-coer"/>) and the contraction rate for 
source iteration (<xref ref="thm-conv-l2"/> and 
<xref ref="cor-conv-supg"/>) both rely on the single 
constraint
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-cs-condition">
    \label{eqn:Cs-condition}
    C_s = \frac{\linf{\sigma_T}\,\ldouble{\mathcal{K}}}{\mu}
    \left(1 + (\mu\,\delta_{\max})^{1/2}\right)
    \;\leq\; \tfrac{1}{2}.
</md>
This is a subcriticality condition on the scattering 
operator: it requires the scattering cross section 
<m>\sigma_T</m>, kernel mass <m>\ldouble{\mathcal{K}}</m>, and 
stabilisation parameter <m>\delta_{\max}</m> to be small relative 
to the positivity constant <m>\mu</m>. Physically, this refers to
absorption effects sufficiently dominating over scattering. 
    </p>

    <p>
Second, the coercivity proof for <m>a</m> (<xref ref="lem-a-coer"/>) requires
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-delta-condition">
    \label{eqn:delta-condition}
    \delta_{\max} \;\leq\; \frac{\mu}{\linf{\sigma}^2},
</md>
which prevents the indefinite SUPG cross term 
<m>\delta_K \sigma v\,(\mathbf{b}\cdot\nabla v)</m> from 
overwhelming the streamline diffusion contribution. The 
standard SUPG scaling <m>\delta_K = h_K</m> used in our numerical 
experiments satisfies<xref ref="eqn-delta-condition"/> for 
sufficiently fine meshes, namely 
<m>h \leq \mu/\linf{\sigma}^2</m>.
    </p>


</subsection>

</section>